# Next.js / Nuxt patterns

Idiomatic meta-framework design for Next.js's App Router and Nuxt 3+ —
routing conventions, rendering-mode choice, and data fetching, covering
both where their concerns diverge and where they converge. Narrows
`core-common-rules`; framework-agnostic design patterns still come from
there.

## Server/client boundary (Next.js) and setup context (Nuxt)

- Fetch data in Server Components (no `'use client'`) by default; push
  `'use client'` only to the interactive leaf (a button, a form, a chart
  that needs a browser API) so the rest of the tree stays server-rendered
  and out of the client bundle.
- Pass data down from a Server Component to a Client Component as plain
  serializable props — a Client Component cannot `await` a server-only
  data call itself; if it needs fresh data after mount, call a Route
  Handler (`app/api/.../route.ts`) or a Server Action, not a re-implemented
  server-only fetch client-side.
- A Server Action is declared with `'use server'` (file-level or inline in
  an async function) and can be called directly from a form's `action`
  prop or a Client Component's event handler; mutate, then call
  `revalidatePath()`/`revalidateTag()` so the affected route re-renders
  with fresh data in the same round trip — don't hand-roll a client-side
  refetch-after-mutation when a Server Action + revalidate already covers
  it.
- Nuxt composables (`useX`) must be called synchronously during a
  component's `setup()` (top level of `<script setup>`), a plugin, or
  middleware — never inside an `async` callback, a `setTimeout`, or after
  an `await` inside setup, because Nuxt resolves a composable's injection
  context (route, app, Pinia store) synchronously at call time.

## Rendering modes

- Next.js: a route is static by default (build-time render, cached);
  reading `cookies()`/`headers()`/a `searchParams` prop, or an uncached
  `fetch`, opts it into dynamic per-request rendering. Use
  `export const revalidate = <seconds>` for ISR when the data is allowed
  to go stale for a bounded window rather than forcing full dynamic
  rendering.
- Nuxt: `ssr: true` (default) renders on each request; `routeRules` in
  `nuxt.config.ts` set per-route rendering (`{ '/blog/**': { isr: 3600 } }`
  for ISR-like caching, `{ '/app/**': { ssr: false } }` for an SPA-only
  section, `{ '/': { prerender: true } }` for static). Choose the rule per
  route instead of setting `ssr: false` globally when only part of the app
  needs it.
- Both frameworks: a component that needs data on first paint should fetch
  it where the framework already renders (Server Component / `useFetch`),
  not fetch client-side after an empty shell — that trade-off (an extra
  client round trip, a loading flash) should be a deliberate choice, not
  the default from not knowing the server-fetch path exists.

## Data fetching

- Next.js: `fetch()` inside a Server Component is deduplicated and cached
  by the framework across a single render pass — prefer it over a
  third-party data-fetching library for simple server-side reads unless
  the project already standardizes on one (e.g. a query library for
  client-side cache/mutation needs).
- Nuxt: use `useFetch`/`useAsyncData` for data a page needs during SSR
  (they dedupe across server and client and hydrate without a second
  request); a plain `$fetch` call inside `onMounted` only re-fetches
  client-side and loses SSR — use it for a genuinely client-only,
  post-interaction call (e.g. in response to a click), not initial page
  data.
- Both: key async data explicitly (`useAsyncData('orders', ...)` in Nuxt;
  a stable cache key in whatever client-side query library Next.js code
  uses) — an unkeyed or auto-keyed-by-reference call can silently refetch
  or fail to dedupe across re-renders/navigations.

## Anti-patterns

- Marking an entire page or layout `'use client'` to fix one interactive
  child, instead of isolating the directive to that child.
- Reaching for `ssr: false` on a whole Nuxt page to work around a
  hydration mismatch instead of finding the actual nondeterministic
  render source.
- Storing derived/computed state in a `ref`/`useState` and syncing it with
  an effect/`watch`, instead of computing it inline with `computed()` or
  during render — both frameworks' reactivity systems already handle
  derivation without an effect.
